WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:01.126
We've talked here before

00:00:01.126 --> 00:00:02.919
about the health care disparities

00:00:02.919 --> 00:00:04.087
that minority adults

00:00:04.087 --> 00:00:06.673
have come to expect in this country.

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:08.049
Well, now there's a new report

00:00:08.049 --> 00:00:09.134
that children

00:00:09.134 --> 00:00:10.677
of all minority groups

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:13.013
also face disparities in care.

00:00:13.013 --> 00:00:14.139
In a review of studies

00:00:14.139 --> 00:00:15.098
done over a five year

00:00:15.098 --> 00:00:16.016
period, researchers

00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:17.559
found that overall,

00:00:17.559 --> 00:00:18.727
the literature reveals

00:00:18.727 --> 00:00:20.145
widespread patterns

00:00:20.145 --> 00:00:21.688
of inequitable treatment

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:23.857
across pediatric specialties.

00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:25.525
Those include specialties,

00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:26.818
everything from neonatal

00:00:26.818 --> 00:00:29.195
care to end of life care.

00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:30.613
Joining me now to talk about her

00:00:30.613 --> 00:00:31.489
findings is Dr.

00:00:31.489 --> 00:00:32.657
Nia Hurd Garrett.

00:00:32.657 --> 00:00:34.492
She's a pediatrician and researcher

00:00:34.492 --> 00:00:35.827
who oversaw the review.

00:00:35.827 --> 00:00:38.663
Thank you so much for being with us

00:00:38.663 --> 00:00:40.999
from from neonatal care,

00:00:40.999 --> 00:00:42.459
from the beginning of life

00:00:42.459 --> 00:00:43.793
to the end of life

00:00:43.793 --> 00:00:46.755
and across all specialties.

00:00:46.755 --> 00:00:49.716
What did you find?

00:00:49.966 --> 00:00:50.383
Yes.

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:52.552
From the very first moments of life,

00:00:52.552 --> 00:00:54.763
all the way to the last moments of life

00:00:54.763 --> 00:00:56.264
for children,

00:00:56.264 --> 00:00:57.682
we're seeing disparities

00:00:57.682 --> 00:01:00.310
in almost every aspect of pediatric care.

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:01.603
And as a pediatrician

00:01:01.603 --> 00:01:03.563
and as a mom, it's pretty disheartening.

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:05.690
But every almost every single specialty

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:08.276
we have, we found disparities.

00:01:08.276 --> 00:01:10.320
And the one that shows one of the greater

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.156
disparities is in pain management.

00:01:13.156 --> 00:01:16.409
If a kid breaks his arm tumbling

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:19.746
or sprains an ankle, why roller skating?

00:01:19.829 --> 00:01:20.955
Children of color

00:01:20.955 --> 00:01:22.957
are less likely to get those painkillers.

00:01:22.957 --> 00:01:25.502
Explain the disparity here.

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:26.211
Absolutely.

00:01:26.211 --> 00:01:27.962
There's been so much research,

00:01:27.962 --> 00:01:29.089
especially in the emergency

00:01:29.089 --> 00:01:30.256
room department,

00:01:30.256 --> 00:01:31.758
about pain management

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:32.926
in minorities, youth.

00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:34.928
So black and Hispanic

00:01:34.928 --> 00:01:36.221
youth are less likely

00:01:36.221 --> 00:01:37.430
to get the pain medicine

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:38.807
that they need despite

00:01:38.807 --> 00:01:39.641
having a bone

00:01:39.641 --> 00:01:42.644
fracture, appendicitis, migraine

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:44.687
and even those that are getting a finger

00:01:44.687 --> 00:01:45.105
or toe

00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:47.023
replaced are less likely

00:01:47.023 --> 00:01:48.316
to get pain medicine.

00:01:48.316 --> 00:01:50.944
And that is absolutely unacceptable.

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:53.655
Is it clear why?

00:01:53.655 --> 00:01:56.116
So we think a lot of these disparities

00:01:56.116 --> 00:01:57.909
and and inequities that we see

00:01:57.909 --> 00:01:58.368
are driven

00:01:58.368 --> 00:01:59.661
by structural racism

00:01:59.661 --> 00:02:01.788
and implicit bias and other factors

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:02.747
that are pervasive

00:02:02.747 --> 00:02:04.374
not only in our society,

00:02:04.374 --> 00:02:06.334
but also in our health care systems.

00:02:06.334 --> 00:02:08.044
We need to do more research to understand

00:02:08.044 --> 00:02:09.712
exactly why we're seeing this.

00:02:09.712 --> 00:02:10.922
But one of the things we did in

00:02:10.922 --> 00:02:11.631
our study

00:02:11.631 --> 00:02:13.091
is taking out the

00:02:13.091 --> 00:02:15.343
the argument that it's only insurance.

00:02:15.343 --> 00:02:16.636
The reason why people aren't

00:02:16.636 --> 00:02:17.887
getting the care that they need

00:02:17.887 --> 00:02:19.389
is because they don't have insurance.

00:02:19.389 --> 00:02:21.349
And that is not true for the study.

00:02:21.349 --> 00:02:22.934
We controlled for that.

00:02:22.934 --> 00:02:23.852
That means we made sure

00:02:23.852 --> 00:02:26.146
that all of these children had insurance

00:02:26.146 --> 00:02:28.606
and we're still seeing these disparities.

00:02:28.606 --> 00:02:30.817
So they all had insurance.

00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:32.318
Are there economic levels

00:02:32.318 --> 00:02:34.070
in which they are treated differently

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:35.780
based on the communities

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:37.282
in which they live or

00:02:37.282 --> 00:02:40.368
the income of their families?

00:02:40.451 --> 00:02:41.411
Absolutely.

00:02:41.411 --> 00:02:42.162
There there needs

00:02:42.162 --> 00:02:43.580
to be more work in this space

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:44.706
because we know that

00:02:44.706 --> 00:02:47.083
even though children have insurance,

00:02:47.083 --> 00:02:49.169
all insurance is not created equal.

00:02:49.169 --> 00:02:50.753
So if you have public insurance

00:02:50.753 --> 00:02:52.046
or you have commercial insurance

00:02:52.046 --> 00:02:53.047
or private insurance,

00:02:53.047 --> 00:02:54.007
there are still levels

00:02:54.007 --> 00:02:55.592
within those insurances.

00:02:55.592 --> 00:02:56.926
So there is possibilities

00:02:56.926 --> 00:02:57.969
that even because

00:02:57.969 --> 00:02:59.387
even though you have insurance,

00:02:59.387 --> 00:03:00.180
doesn't mean you're going

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.931
to get the top quality care.

00:03:01.931 --> 00:03:03.766
But even despite that,

00:03:03.766 --> 00:03:04.851
even looking at those

00:03:04.851 --> 00:03:06.853
that only had Medicaid insurance

00:03:06.853 --> 00:03:08.646
or only had private insurance,

00:03:08.646 --> 00:03:11.107
those disparities by race and ethnicity

00:03:11.107 --> 00:03:12.233
still persisted.

00:03:12.233 --> 00:03:13.860
So it didn't really matter

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.069
that you only had

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:16.738
a certain type of insurance.

00:03:16.738 --> 00:03:17.864
What seem to really be

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:20.992
the drivers here were race and ethnicity.

00:03:21.242 --> 00:03:23.411
And that is, again, a very unfortunate.

00:03:23.411 --> 00:03:24.871
And the pediatrician and a mom.

00:03:24.871 --> 00:03:25.663
It certainly is.

00:03:25.663 --> 00:03:28.291
And last for the moms

00:03:28.291 --> 00:03:30.210
who are watching and dads to

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:31.961
what do they do with this?

00:03:31.961 --> 00:03:33.046
Well, how do they

00:03:33.046 --> 00:03:34.422
then advocate for

00:03:34.422 --> 00:03:36.799
care for their children?

00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:37.550
Absolutely.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:38.051
I think there

00:03:38.051 --> 00:03:40.261
needs to be a multi-level approach

00:03:40.261 --> 00:03:40.720
to this.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.056
I don't want there to read this study

00:03:43.056 --> 00:03:43.848
and just leave

00:03:43.848 --> 00:03:46.100
with a heavy heart and say, what do I do?

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:47.227
You know, my kid needs care.

00:03:47.227 --> 00:03:48.436
I need help

00:03:48.436 --> 00:03:50.188
and then be scared to interact

00:03:50.188 --> 00:03:51.522
with the health care system.

00:03:51.522 --> 00:03:52.649
So there are things that I think

00:03:52.649 --> 00:03:54.192
parents and caregivers can do,

00:03:54.192 --> 00:03:55.777
advocating for their children.

00:03:55.777 --> 00:03:56.653
You know your child

00:03:56.653 --> 00:03:57.695
best and trying to push

00:03:57.695 --> 00:03:58.988
for the care that they need.

00:03:58.988 --> 00:03:59.530
But it shouldn't

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:01.366
only be on the patient and family.

00:04:01.366 --> 00:04:02.450
Our health care systems

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:03.910
need to review their policies

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:05.078
and look at their data

00:04:05.078 --> 00:04:06.955
to make sure that we're not exacerbating

00:04:06.955 --> 00:04:08.331
or making disparities

00:04:08.331 --> 00:04:10.208
inequities worse for kids

00:04:10.208 --> 00:04:11.376
and then for society.

00:04:11.376 --> 00:04:13.211
Policymakers and policy.

00:04:13.211 --> 00:04:14.212
Politicians

00:04:14.212 --> 00:04:14.671
can help

00:04:14.671 --> 00:04:16.547
to make these inequities better

00:04:16.547 --> 00:04:18.132
by investing in communities

00:04:18.132 --> 00:04:19.425
and making sure there are good,

00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:22.095
solid systems for children and families.

00:04:22.095 --> 00:04:22.470
Dr.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:23.846
Nia Heard Garris,

00:04:23.846 --> 00:04:25.223
thank you for doing the work

00:04:25.223 --> 00:04:26.641
and thank you for the conversation.

